00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:06.980
LILIANA YU: Ryan
Shatford, previously

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.070
the front-end and edge site
reliability engineer at Uber,

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.700
had this to say
about Stackdriver.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.810
"Stackdriver is a
quick and easy utility

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.850
to monitor your
app's performance.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.620
Within minutes,
we had dashboarbs

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.920
for compute instances
and VPN statistics.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.960
An easy to use
policy based alert

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.320
management system helps us
notify on-call engineers

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.880
of issues quickly."

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.470
We're happy you like it, Ryan.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:31.950
So do we.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.400
Google Stackdriver provides
powerful monitoring, logging,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.710
and diagnostics.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.720
It equips you with insight
into the health, performance,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.740
and availability of
Cloud-powered applications,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.070
enabling you to find
and fix issues faster.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.310
It is natively integrated with
Google Cloud Platform, Amazon

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:52.640
Web Services, and popular
open source packages.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.480
Stackdriver provides a wide
variety of metrics, dashboards,

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.990
alerting, log management,
reporting, and tracing

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.820
capabilities.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.090
This hands-on Qwiklab will
lead you through creating a VM,

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.080
installing Apache 2 on it.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.680
And it will show you how
to create an uptime check

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.410
on Stackdriver, create
an alerting policy

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.990
for your uptime check,
create a dashboard

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.090
and chart for a CPU
load, stop your VM

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.870
and view the incident
noticed in Stackdriver,

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:24.920
and view the logs for your
VM with Stackdriver Logging.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.490
As with all our labs, you do
not need a Google Cloud Platform

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.050
account or project.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.660
An account, project,
and associated resources

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.180
are provided to you
as part of this lab.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.640
If you already have
your own GCP account,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.660
don't use it for this lab.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.260
You're one lab away from
completing your quest

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.550
and getting your badge.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.280
In case you haven't been
following our other videos,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.440
a quest is a series
of related labs

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.780
that form a learning
path on Qwiklabs.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.630
Completing this quest
earns you this badge

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.650
to recognize your achievement.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.740
The next and final
lab on your quest

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.300
is Setting Up Network
and HTTP Load Balances.

